// scaling_trajectory.vsm — where the framework can GROW (a NOT-NOW consideration).
// author: rmw3 (S5, direction) + Fable (mapping) · 2026-07-04.
// NOT a spec to implement. A captured trajectory: an organization with thousands of workers + hundreds of
// specialized AI agents, role-based permissions, isolation. Purpose = ensure the direction is CONSIDERED,
// so nothing we build now closes a door we'll want open later.

trajectory(VSF_AT_ORGANIZATIONAL_SCALE) {

  core_insight:
    "The scaling story is ALREADY in the architecture — it IS the recursion. Beer designed VSM for exactly
     this (Cybersyn was meant to run a whole national economy). Growing to thousands of workers + hundreds of
     agents does NOT require re-architecting — it requires instantiating the recursion DEEPER, under the SAME
     invariants. That is the point of a domain-agnostic, recursive certifier: no discontinuity of mechanism
     between a 2-node system and a 10,000-node one."

  // ── how the requested capabilities MAP to what we already have ────────────────
  maps_to_existing: {
    thousands_of_workers:
      "= more RECURSION LEVELS. A team is an S1; a department is an S1 whose S1s are teams; a division likewise.
       Variety is ATTENUATED up (φ↑) and policy AMPLIFIED down (φ↓) at each level — so the top never drowns in
       thousands of workers; it sees the attenuated summary from the level below. Ashby / the opsroom at each level.",
    hundreds_of_agents:
      "= ATTESTED S1 nodes. Each agent is admitted via the beacon EVC gate (calibrate-before-trust + signed
       attestation), runs under the covenant floor + R10.1 (means, not ends) + the escalation duty. Hundreds of
       agents ≠ hundreds of ungoverned actors — each is the SAME governed node, verifiable up the chain.",
    role_based_permissions:
      "= STRUCTURAL, not an ad-hoc ACL. Who-can-do-what derives from (a) POSITION in the recursion and (b) which
       VSM FUNCTION you serve: an S1 operator acts within pre-authorized bounds; an S3 allocates to its S1s; an S5
       sets ends for its level. Cleaner than typical RBAC because it FOLLOWS the viability structure. The signing
       PKI is recursive too — each level's authority holds an operational key issued by the level above's CA.",
    specialized_isolated_agents:
      "= S1s with their OWN crash domain (the 'new S1 gets its own LXC' rule) + A4 (NO direct sibling coupling —
       all communication via the parent S3). This is what PREVENTS the 'hundreds of agents wired to everything'
       chaos: the recursion CHANNELS communication; isolation is structural, not bolted on.",
    failure_at_scale:
      "= recursive algedonic cascade. Pain at level N that N cannot resolve escalates to N+1 (the P5 banking
       example already shows this). A problem in one of thousands of units surfaces UP to the level that can act.",
  }

  // ── the invariants that MUST hold at ANY scale (non-negotiable) ───────────────
  invariants_at_scale: [
    "A0 / A0.1 — the covenant (ethical primacy + anti-capture) is inviolable at every level. Sub-levels may add
     STRICTER local policy; NEVER looser (monotone toward the root).",
    "A5 — human authority ceiling: double-loop change requires a human signature at the responsible level.",
    "calibrate-before-trust — no node participates until its gauges DISCRIMINATE (Axis-1) + it attests.",
    "model≡reality — the census reconciles declared vs running at every level (anti-orphan, both directions).",
    "the signed authority chain — every autonomous action attributable to a human key up the recursion.",
  ]

  // ── the OPEN questions we will need to solve (honest, not naive) ──────────────
  open_questions: [
    "KEY MANAGEMENT at scale — the offline root delegating operational keys across hundreds of nodes: a real
     PKI + rotation + revocation problem. The recursive CA (each level issues to its children) is the shape; the
     operational detail is unsolved.",
    "HUMAN AUTHORITY DISTRIBUTION — not one human signing everything. Each level's S5 signs for its level; S5 may
     be a human OR a higher-trust DELEGATED authority. Defining who-is-S5-where (and how delegation stays covenant-
     bound) is the central governance design of scale.",
    "COVENANT FEDERATION — one root covenant + stricter local policies; how sub-orgs express local ends without
     violating the root. (A0 monotonicity.)",
    "CROSS-CUTTING CONCERNS — security / compliance cut across the recursion; likely a recursive S3* (audit) at
     each level rather than a separate hierarchy.",
    "INFRASTRUCTURE — thousands of nodes = distributed-systems + multi-tenant isolation engineering, far beyond
     the current single-Proxmox host. The MODEL is ready; the substrate is not.",
    "THE HUMAN-VARIETY CEILING — even with attenuation, how deep can one human's strategic supervision reach
     before it must delegate? This is von Foerster's semantic cap at organizational scale.",
  ]

  what_this_means_now:
    "We do NOT define any of this now. But two things follow: (1) do not build anything that assumes a FLAT,
     single-level, single-human system — keep every mechanism recursion-aware (the opsroom, the beacon, the
     coupling, the decision interface all already are). (2) This trajectory IS the full extent of P5's
     recursive_governance pillar — the L1/L2/L3 banking example generalizes to arbitrary depth. The paper can
     point here as the horizon."

  status: "TRAJECTORY captured — a considered direction, not a plan. Revisit when a real multi-team deployment
           (or a domain collaborator) makes the operational detail necessary."
  bridges: "recursive_vsm_theory.md · project_coupling_manifest (DSN-COUPLE-01) · beacon_activation.spec.vsm ·
            supervision_layer.spec.vsm · s1_lifecycle_coupling.spec.vsm · P5 recursive_governance · A4/A5/A0."
}

